<body>

<header>
  <div class="eyebrow">Entrepreneurship · Browning High School</div>
  <h1>Who Is An Entrepreneur?</h1>
  <div class="sub">Week 1 · Day 1 — Unit I: Entrepreneurs &amp; Entrepreneurial Skills</div>
</header>

<div class="timestrip">
  <div class="ts-login">0:00–8:00<br>Login + Typing</div>
  <div class="ts-review">8:00–11:00<br>Opening Hook</div>
  <div class="ts-di">11:00–29:00<br>DI Instruction</div>
  <div class="ts-practice">29:00–40:00<br>Practice / SPED Pull-Aside</div>
  <div class="ts-exit">40:00–45:00<br>Exit + Logoff</div>
</div>

<div class="wrap">

<div class="note goal">
  <span class="label">Unit Goal Connection</span>
  Feeds the Week 1 end artifact — student self-inventory of entrepreneurial traits — which becomes raw material for the Week 5 pitch. Today students meet the concept of Key Resources (their own skills/network) and Key Activities (what entrepreneurs actually do), the two BMG blocks this week builds.
</div>

<h2 class="section">0:00–8:00 · Login &amp; Typing Practice</h2>
<p>Standard routine. Students log in (GradYear+FirstName+LastInitial@bps.k12.mt.us) and open type.com. No new instruction needed today — this block runs on autopilot while you take attendance and get materials ready.</p>

<h2 class="section">8:00–11:00 · Opening Hook</h2>

<div class="note iefa">
  <span class="label">IEFA Connection</span>
  This is the identity-affirming opener before the "Western business" framing begins. Entrepreneurship is not a new concept being introduced to Blackfeet students — it's a lens being put on something they already know. Trading, ranching, guiding, artisan and beadwork sale, seasonal labor — all of it is entrepreneurial activity that already exists in their families and community.
</div>

<div class="script">
  <p class="t">Before we define anything, I want you to think about someone in your own family or community who runs something — could be a small business, could be guiding, ranching, selling beadwork or art, anything where they're the one making it happen. Turn and tell your neighbor who that person is and what they do.</p>
  <p class="a">Give 60 seconds. Walk the room. Listen for names, not just job titles — you want them to say "my auntie" or "my grandpa," not just "a rancher."</p>
  <p class="t">Who wants to share who they thought of?</p>
  <p class="a">Take 2–3 shares. Do not correct or reframe — just receive. You will connect this back at the end of the hook.</p>
  <p class="t">Here's the thing — everyone you just described? That's an entrepreneur. Trading, ranching, guiding, making and selling art — that's not new to us. It's not something we're importing today. We're just going to put a name and some structure on something Blackfeet people have always done.</p>
</div>

<div class="note channel">
  <span class="label">Learning Channel</span>
  Hear/Say (turn-and-talk) into Hear/Do (share-out). This is intentionally low-stakes and social before any vocabulary is introduced — gets everyone talking before anyone has to be "right."
</div>

<h2 class="section">11:00–29:00 · DI Instruction — I Do / We Do / You Do</h2>

<h3 class="subsection">I Do — Defining Entrepreneur</h3>

<div class="script">
  <p class="t">An <span class="k">entrepreneur</span> is someone who starts and runs their own venture — takes on the risk, and keeps the reward or the loss. Say that with me:</p>
  <p class="cr">"An entrepreneur takes on the risk, and keeps the reward or the loss."</p>
  <p class="a">Signal for choral response — point, don't just wait.</p>
  <p class="t">Here's what matters today: an entrepreneur's biggest resource isn't money, and it isn't a fancy office. It's <span class="k">Key Resources</span> — their own skills, and who they know. And what they actually spend their time doing at the start — the calling, the building, the asking — that's called <span class="k">Key Activities</span>.</p>
</div>

<div class="note goal">
  <span class="label">Unit Goal Connection</span>
  This is the direct instructional moment for the Backwards Design goal: students name personal characteristics/skills of successful entrepreneurs and begin to see their own skills and network as Key Resources — not something they need to acquire from nowhere.
</div>

<h3 class="subsection">We Do — Sorting Traits</h3>

<div class="script">
  <p class="t">I'm going to read some traits out loud. If you think this is something an entrepreneur needs, give me a thumbs up. If you don't think so, thumbs down. Ready?</p>
  <p class="a">Read one at a time, pause for signal, briefly discuss any split votes: <span class="k">persistence, waiting to be told what to do, comfort asking for help, needing everything to be perfect before starting, noticing problems other people walk past, fear of being told no.</span></p>
  <p class="t">Notice something — a lot of you already do some of these things. Persistence. Noticing problems. Asking for help when you need it. That's not a coincidence.</p>
</div>

<h3 class="subsection">You Do — Guided Practice</h3>

<div class="script">
  <p class="t">In your workbook, Unit 1 Day 1 page — I want three things: a business or venture you know of in this community, one entrepreneurial trait the person running it clearly has, and one trait from today's list that you already have yourself.</p>
  <p class="a">Circulate. This is the bridge into the independent self-inventory below — watch for students who default to "I don't have any of these" and redirect them back to the opening hook example.</p>
</div>

<div class="note adhd">
  <span class="label">ADHD Observation Tip</span>
  The thumbs up/down sort is fast-moving by design — good fit for working-memory load and impulsivity, since there's no wrong answer waiting period. Watch for students who rush ahead into the You Do before the We Do discussion lands; a quiet tap on the workbook page (not a verbal redirect) is usually enough to bring them back.
</div>

<div class="note autism">
  <span class="label">Autism Observation Tip</span>
  Some students may want a logical reason before giving a thumbs up/down on a trait — that's not stalling, that's genuine processing. If a student pauses on "needing everything to be perfect before starting," it's fine to let them explain their thumbs-down reasoning to the class; it's often more insightful than the intended answer.
</div>

<h2 class="section">29:00–40:00 · Guided/Independent Practice — Two Tracks Running Concurrently</h2>

<h3 class="subsection">Main Class — Independent Practice</h3>
<div class="workbook-box write-area">
  <span class="wb-label">Self-Inventory — 3 Entrepreneurial Traits I Already Have</span>
  Write-in lines for three traits, each with one sentence of "how I know this is true about me." (This is the end-of-week artifact — keep it in the running Week 1–9 workbook section; it feeds the Week 5 pitch.)
</div>

<div class="choice-moment">
  <span class="cm-label">Choice Moment</span>
  Students choose whether to write about a trait they already use at school, at home, or in a cultural/community role (caring for elders or siblings, ceremonial responsibility, animal or land work). Genuinely open — the point is that the "proof" can come from anywhere in their life, not just a classroom.
</div>

<div class="sped-block">
  <h3 class="subsection">SPED Pull-Aside — "What I Need" (Key Resources, Tangible)</h3>
  <p style="margin-top:0;"><strong>Format:</strong> One small group, teacher-led. 2nd-grade reading level. Runs during the block above, concurrent with main class independent practice.</p>

  <div class="sped-script">
    <p class="t">We are going to think about tools. A tool is something you need to do a job.</p>
    <p class="a">Show a real, physical object — a hammer, a spoon, a backpack.</p>
    <p class="t">What do I need this for?</p>
    <p class="s">[names the job the tool does]</p>
    <p class="t">People are tools too — skills are tools. Show me on your face: does this sound like something you're good at? 🙂 or 🙁</p>
    <p class="a">Read each item slowly, one at a time, pause for the smiley-face response before moving on: <em>helping someone, fixing something, talking to new people, taking care of an animal.</em></p>
  </div>

  <div class="workbook-box" style="background:#fff; border-color: var(--accent-green);">
    <span class="wb-label" style="color:#2f7a4a;">Graphic Organizer — My Skills vs. My Chores</span>
    Two columns, picture-supported: "A job I do at home" / "A skill this takes." Student draws or circles a picture rather than writing a sentence. Extended time; no time pressure on this page.
  </div>

  <div class="note comprehension" style="margin-top:14px;">
    <span class="label">Comprehension Tip</span>
    Watch for students copying the smiley face their neighbor drew rather than reflecting on their own answer — this is a comprehension gap, not defiance. Cover neighboring workbooks lightly with a folder if needed, and re-ask the question one-on-one.
  </div>
</div>

<h2 class="section">40:00–45:00 · Exit Check + Logoff</h2>

<div class="script">
  <p class="t">Before you log off — one sentence, out loud, no writing needed: name one entrepreneur trait you have. Just one. I'll go around fast.</p>
  <p class="a">This is a verbal fluency check, not a grade — you're listening for whether the concept landed, not correctness. Log per student: Not Yet / Developing / Fluent.</p>
</div>

<table class="assess">
  <tr><th>Student</th><th>Not Yet</th><th>Developing</th><th>Fluent</th></tr>
  <tr><td colspan="4" style="color:#888;">(roster rows — printed version)</td></tr>
</table>

<div class="identity">
  <h3>You Are Already Someone Who Notices</h3>
  <p>
    <span class="cap">Important names are capitalized</span> — and you are an important person. Today wasn't about becoming an entrepreneur. It was about noticing you already are one — in your family, in this community, in the things you already do without being asked. That doesn't go away when you log off. You carry it into tomorrow's lesson.
  </p>
</div>

</div>
</body>
</html>
