<div class="note goal">
  <span class="label">Unit Goal Connection</span>
  Yesterday introduced Key Resources. Today adds Key Activities, and — new for this rebuild — Channels and Key Partnerships, using two real local ventures instead of a video. Replaces the Crash Course video entirely; no video/AV dependency this lesson.
</div>

<div class="note iefa">
  <span class="label">IEFA / Local Connection</span>
  Both businesses used in today's case study are real and local — this keeps the "entrepreneurship is not new to us" thread from Day 1 running through the whole week instead of just the opener.
</div>

<h2 class="section">0:00–8:00 · Login &amp; Typing Practice</h2>
<p>Standard routine.</p>

<h2 class="section">8:00–11:00 · Choral Recall</h2>
<div class="script">
  <p class="t">Yesterday we defined entrepreneur. Say it with me:</p>
  <p class="cr">"An entrepreneur takes on the risk, and keeps the reward or the loss."</p>
  <p class="t">And their biggest resource isn't money — it's their <span class="k">Key Resources</span>. What are Key Resources?</p>
  <p class="cr">"Skills. And who you know."</p>
  <p class="t">Today we're going to see two real, local entrepreneurs, and they didn't grow the same way. Watch for what's different.</p>
</div>

<h2 class="section">11:00–29:00 · DI Instruction — Live Case Study</h2>

<h3 class="subsection">I Do — Case 1: Rock'N Outlaw</h3>
<div class="script">
  <p class="t">Rock'N Outlaw started very small — flowers, perfume, jewelry. That's it. Small products, small footprint.</p>
  <p class="a">Ask: what were their Key Resources at the start? Take answers — expect: knowledge of the products, an eye for what customers want, willingness to start small.</p>
  <p class="t">Here's what happened next — they grew. They secured a loan and are expanding into a 30 by 60 foot building, and they're adding a whole new segment: animal feed. That makes them start to look a lot like Murdoch's.</p>
  <p class="t">A loan is a <span class="k">Key Resource</span> too — it's not a skill, it's capital. Growing bigger sometimes means bringing in a resource you didn't start with.</p>
  <p class="a">Ask: what changed about their Customer Segment when they added feed? (from gift-shoppers to ranchers/farmers needing supplies — a broader, different customer.)</p>
</div>

<h3 class="subsection">I Do — Case 2: The Moccasin Maker at Faught's</h3>
<div class="script">
  <p class="t">Now — a moccasin maker. Skilled hands, makes a real product. But this person didn't take out a loan or build their own store. They sell on <span class="k">consignment</span> at Faught's — an existing store that already has customers walking through the door.</p>
  <p class="a">Ask: what's this entrepreneur's Key Resource? (expect: the craft/skill itself, not money.) What's different about how they reach customers compared to Rock'N Outlaw?</p>
  <p class="t">Rock'N Outlaw built their own building to reach customers. The moccasin maker uses somebody else's store. In business terms, that's called a <span class="k">Channel</span> — how you actually get your product in front of people. And the relationship with Faught's that makes that possible — that's a <span class="k">Key Partnership</span>.</p>
</div>

<h3 class="subsection">We Do — Naming the Difference</h3>
<div class="script">
  <p class="t">Same question for both — say it with me:</p>
  <p class="cr">"Two entrepreneurs, two different paths."</p>
  <p class="t">Rock'N Outlaw's Channel is...</p>
  <p class="s">[their own store]</p>
  <p class="t">The moccasin maker's Channel is...</p>
  <p class="s">[consignment through Faught's]</p>
  <p class="t">Which one needed a loan to grow?</p>
  <p class="s">[Rock'N Outlaw]</p>
  <p class="t">Both are real entrepreneurs. Neither path is the "right" one — they're just different tools for the same job: getting your product to people who want it.</p>
</div>

<div class="note channel">
  <span class="label">Learning Channel</span>
  See/Say into Think/Say — the comparison structure (same two questions asked of both cases back-to-back) builds the habit of analyzing any business against the same BMG blocks, which is exactly the skill this whole pre-midterm sequence is building toward.
</div>

<div class="note comprehension">
  <span class="label">Comprehension Tip</span>
  Watch for students who think Rock'N Outlaw's path is "more successful" just because it's bigger. Push back gently — ask what risk each entrepreneur took on relative to what they had. The moccasin maker took on less financial risk and still built something real; that's not a lesser path, it's a different one.
</div>

<h3 class="subsection">You Do — Guided Practice</h3>
<div class="script">
  <p class="t">In your workbook, fill in the comparison chart — Key Resources, Key Activities, and Channel for both businesses.</p>
</div>

<h2 class="section">29:00–40:00 · Compare Chart — Two Tracks Running Concurrently</h2>

<h3 class="subsection">Main Class — Comparison Chart</h3>
<div class="compare">
  <div class="col">
    <h4>Rock'N Outlaw</h4>
    Key Resources: ____________<br>
    Key Activities: ____________<br>
    Channel: ____________
  </div>
  <div class="col">
    <h4>Moccasin Maker / Faught's</h4>
    Key Resources: ____________<br>
    Key Activities: ____________<br>
    Channel: ____________
  </div>
</div>
<p>Early finishers: which path sounds more like something you'd want to try, and why? (No wrong answer — feeds Day 4.)</p>

<div class="sped-block">
  <h3 class="subsection">SPED Pull-Aside — "Big Store or Someone Else's Store?"</h3>
  <p style="margin-top:0;"><strong>Format:</strong> One small group, teacher-led, 2nd-grade reading level.</p>
  <div class="sped-script">
    <p class="t">One business built a big new building. One business sells inside somebody else's store. Both are real businesses.</p>
    <p class="a">Use two picture cards: a big building, and a smaller shelf/rack inside another store.</p>
    <p class="t">Rock'N Outlaw got bigger — they needed a loan, which means borrowed money. The moccasin maker didn't need a loan. They just needed their skill and a place to sell — Faught's said yes.</p>
    <p class="a">Sort simple picture cards of local business types into "Has Their Own Building" vs. "Sells Inside Another Store." No wrong answers — the point is recognizing both are real businesses.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="note comprehension" style="margin-top:14px;">
    <span class="label">Comprehension Tip</span>
    If a student assumes the bigger building means "better business," gently redirect: "Both people are working. Both people are selling something people want." Keep the emphasis on effort and skill, not size.
  </div>
</div>

<h2 class="section">40:00–45:00 · Exit Check</h2>
<div class="script">
  <p class="t">One sentence: name one way Rock'N Outlaw and the moccasin maker are different, and one way they're the same.</p>
  <p class="a">Not Yet / Developing / Fluent per student.</p>
</div>

