<h2 class="section">11:00–29:00 · Value Mini-Lesson + Fluency Rounds</h2>

<div class="note iefa">
  <span class="label">IEFA Connection — Dentalium Trade Currency</span>
  The Yurok, Karuk, and Hupa peoples of the lower Klamath and Trinity River region used dentalium shells — long, hollow, tusk-shaped mollusk shells (Dentalium pretiosum, sometimes called "tusk shells") — as a primary form of currency and a measure of wealth. The shells were sourced through extensive trade networks stretching north to Vancouver Island, and their value was graded by length, often measured against tattooed marks on a trader's hand or forearm — longer shells were worth more. Dentalium was used to buy goods, mark social standing, and settle serious matters. Verify current, detailed sourcing before presenting this as definitive historical detail to students — this is accurate at the general level but deserves a real citation in the final script.
</div>

<h3 class="subsection">I Do — Why Was a Shell Worth Anything?</h3>
<div class="script">
  <p class="t">Before we drill vocabulary, I want you to think about something. The Yurok, Karuk, and Hupa peoples — nations along the Klamath and Trinity Rivers — used a shell called <span class="k">dentalium</span> as money. Not paper money, not coins. A shell.</p>
  <p class="t">Here's the question: a dentalium shell can't feed you, can't keep you warm, can't do anything on its own. So why was it worth something?</p>
  <p class="a">Take 2–3 guesses before confirming.</p>
  <p class="t">It was rare, it was hard to get — traded down from as far as Vancouver Island — and everyone agreed it was worth something. That's it. That's the whole idea behind value. Something becomes valuable when it's hard to get <span class="k">and</span> people agree on it.</p>
  <p class="t">Now think about Key Resources — your own skills. Same idea. A skill is worth something in a business when it's useful <span class="k">and</span> not everyone has it.</p>
</div>

<div class="note channel">
  <span class="label">Learning Channel</span>
  Think/Say — this mini-lesson is deliberately conceptual before the mechanical fluency drilling starts, so "value" isn't just another vocabulary card, it's a concept students reasoned through first.
</div>

<h3 class="subsection">Precision Teaching — Three-Round Fluency Structure</h3>
<div class="script">
  <p class="t">Today isn't new content. It's getting fast <em>and</em> accurate with everything we already know. Three rounds.</p>
  <p class="a"><span class="k">Round 1 — With Support:</span> Word wall visible (entrepreneur, Key Resources, Key Activities, gig economy, risk, value, trade). Call a term, students respond chorally with the definition, checking the wall as needed.</p>
  <p class="a"><span class="k">Round 2 — Without Support:</span> Cover the word wall. Timed — 60 seconds. Call terms rapid-fire, individual students respond, log response speed and accuracy per student.</p>
</div>

<div class="fluency-table">
  <tr><th>Round</th><th>Support</th><th>Timing</th><th>Response Mode</th></tr>
  <tr><td>1</td><td>Word wall visible</td><td>Untimed</td><td>Choral</td></tr>
  <tr><td>2</td><td>None</td><td>60 sec, timed</td><td>Individual, cold-call</td></tr>
  <tr><td>3</td><td>None</td><td>Untimed</td><td>Peer partner quiz</td></tr>
</div>

<div class="note adhd">
  <span class="label">ADHD Observation Tip</span>
  Round 2's timer can spike impulsivity — students blurting before being called. Keep a visible "hands down, wait for your name" cue rather than a verbal reminder each time; repetitive verbal correction burns working memory faster than a consistent physical signal.
</div>

<h2 class="section">29:00–40:00 · Round 3 — Peer Identification / SPED Pull-Aside</h2>

<h3 class="subsection">Main Class — Round 3</h3>
<div class="script">
  <p class="t">Partner up. One of you quizzes the other on all seven terms. If your partner gets stuck, don't just give them the answer — ask a question that helps them find it themselves.</p>
  <p class="a">This is the error-correction script in peer form — model it once with a volunteer before releasing. Circulate and log Not Yet/Developing/Fluent per student based on Round 2 + Round 3 combined.</p>
</div>

<div class="sped-block">
  <h3 class="subsection">SPED Pull-Aside — "Worth Trading" (concept reinforcement, not timed)</h3>
  <p style="margin-top:0;"><strong>Format:</strong> One small group, teacher-led, 2nd-grade reading level. Repeated practice reps, no stopwatch pressure — precision teaching principle scaled to untimed repetition instead.</p>
  <div class="sped-script">
    <p class="t">Long ago, people traded shells like this for things they needed. It was like money.</p>
    <p class="a">Show a picture or real example of a dentalium/tusk shell if available.</p>
    <p class="t">Was it easy to find, or hard to find?</p>
    <p class="s">[hard to find]</p>
    <p class="t">Right. Hard to find things are worth more. What's something YOU can do that not everyone can do?</p>
    <p class="a">Return to Day 1's smiley-face skill list (helping someone, fixing something, talking to new people, taking care of an animal) — connect: "that skill is like the shell. It's worth something because not everyone has it."</p>
  </div>
</div>

<h2 class="section">40:00–45:00 · Exit Check</h2>
<div class="script">
  <p class="t">Fast round, no writing: I say a term, you say the definition. Last chance to show me fluent before we move on tomorrow.</p>
</div>

