<header>
  <div class="eyebrow">Entrepreneurship · Browning High School</div>
  <h1>What Have I Already Started?</h1>
  <div class="sub">Week 1 · Day 4 — Applying Key Activities to My Own Life</div>
</header>

<div class="timestrip">
  <div class="ts-login">0:00–8:00<br>Login + Typing</div>
  <div class="ts-review">8:00–11:00<br>Choral Recall</div>
  <div class="ts-di">11:00–29:00<br>DI Instruction</div>
  <div class="ts-practice">29:00–40:00<br>Draft Artifact / SPED Pull-Aside</div>
  <div class="ts-exit">40:00–45:00<br>Exit + Logoff</div>
</div>

<div class="wrap">

<div class="note goal">
  <span class="label">Unit Goal Connection</span>
  This is the bridge day — students take everything from Days 1–3 (Key Resources, Key Activities, value) and apply it to their own life directly, drafting the raw material for Friday's finished self-inventory artifact.
</div>

<h2 class="section">0:00–8:00 · Login &amp; Typing Practice</h2>
<p>Standard routine.</p>

<h2 class="section">8:00–11:00 · Choral Recall</h2>
<div class="script">
  <p class="t">This week so far: entrepreneurs have Key Resources — what are those?</p>
  <p class="cr">"Skills. And who you know."</p>
  <p class="t">And Key Activities — what are those?</p>
  <p class="cr">"What they actually do."</p>
  <p class="t">And something is valuable when it's...</p>
  <p class="cr">"Hard to get, and people agree it matters."</p>
  <p class="t">Today we stop talking about other entrepreneurs. We talk about you.</p>
</div>

<h2 class="section">11:00–29:00 · DI Instruction</h2>

<h3 class="subsection">I Do — Naming My Own Key Activities</h3>
<div class="script">
  <p class="t">A Key Activity isn't just "big business stuff." It's anything you already do consistently, that takes effort, and that gets a result. Watch — I'll do mine first.</p>
  <p class="a">Model with a real personal example: e.g., "Every week I plan lessons for four different grade levels. That takes organizing, thinking ahead, and adjusting when something doesn't work. That's a Key Activity."</p>
  <p class="t">Notice I didn't pick something fancy. I picked something real that I actually do.</p>
</div>

<h3 class="subsection">We Do — Class Brainstorm</h3>
<div class="script">
  <p class="t">Call out things people in this room already do consistently — sports practice, watching siblings, working with animals, a part-time job, ceremonial responsibilities, anything.</p>
  <p class="a">Write responses on the board without judgment — this list becomes the word bank for the You Do below. Push past the first 3–4 obvious answers; this is where quieter students often name something no one else thought of.</p>
</div>

<div class="note channel">
  <span class="label">Learning Channel</span>
  Hear/Say into Think/Write — the board brainstorm is intentionally loud and social before the quieter individual writing task, so students aren't staring at a blank page with no starting point.
</div>

<h3 class="subsection">You Do — Guided Practice</h3>
<div class="script">
  <p class="t">Pick one thing from the board — yours or someone else's that also applies to you — and write two sentences: what you do, and what effort it actually takes.</p>
</div>

<div class="note autism">
  <span class="label">Autism Observation Tip</span>
  If a student insists none of the board examples apply to them, don't push a forced match — ask directly, "what's something you do every week that other people don't have to think about?" Literal, specific framing works better than "just pick something."
</div>

<h2 class="section">29:00–40:00 · Draft the Artifact — Two Tracks</h2>

<h3 class="subsection">Main Class — Draft Round for Friday's Artifact</h3>
<div class="workbook-box write-area">
  <span class="wb-label">Draft: My Key Activities</span>
  Two Key Activities, each with the effort it takes and the result it produces. This is rough-draft language — Friday's version gets polished into the final self-inventory tied to entrepreneurial traits.
</div>

<div class="choice-moment">
  <span class="cm-label">Choice Moment</span>
  Students choose whether their two Key Activities come from the same area of life (both from home, both from sports) or two different areas — genuinely open, no "correct" mix.
</div>

<div class="sped-block">
  <h3 class="subsection">SPED Pull-Aside — "What Do I Already Do?"</h3>
  <p style="margin-top:0;"><strong>Format:</strong> One small group, teacher-led, 2nd-grade reading level.</p>
  <div class="sped-script">
    <p class="t">This week we talked about skills and doing things. Today, tell me one thing you do every week.</p>
    <p class="s">[names an activity — chore, sport, helping someone]</p>
    <p class="t">Does that take work?</p>
    <p class="s">[yes/no]</p>
    <p class="t">If it takes work and you do it every week — that's a Key Activity. You already have one.</p>
    <p class="a">Have the student draw a simple picture of themselves doing the activity, label it with one word if able.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<h2 class="section">40:00–45:00 · Exit Check</h2>
<div class="script">
  <p class="t">One sentence: what's one Key Activity you already do, and what result does it get?</p>
  <p class="a">Not Yet / Developing / Fluent per student.</p>
</div>

<table class="assess">
  <tr><th>Student</th><th>Not Yet</th><th>Developing</th><th>Fluent</th></tr>
  <tr><td colspan="4" style="color:#888;">(roster rows — printed version)</td></tr>
</table>

<div class="identity">
  <h3>You've Been Doing This Longer Than You Think</h3>
  <p><span class="cap">Important names are capitalized</span> — and you are an important person. What you named today isn't small. Showing up, doing the work, getting it done — that's the whole engine behind every business that's ever existed. Tomorrow we put it together.</p>
</div>

</div>
</body>
