<header>
  <div class="eyebrow">Entrepreneurship · Browning High School</div>
  <h1>Putting It Together</h1>
  <div class="sub">Week 1 · Day 5 — Synthesis, Portfolio Piece, Preview of Week 2</div>
</header>

<div class="timestrip">
  <div class="ts-login">0:00–8:00<br>Login + Typing</div>
  <div class="ts-review">8:00–11:00<br>Week 1 Recap</div>
  <div class="ts-di">11:00–29:00<br>Finalize Artifact</div>
  <div class="ts-practice">29:00–40:00<br>Share Out / SPED Pull-Aside</div>
  <div class="ts-exit">40:00–45:00<br>Exit + Preview Week 2</div>
</div>

<div class="wrap">

<div class="note goal">
  <span class="label">Unit Goal Connection</span>
  This is the day the Week 1 end-of-week artifact — "3 Entrepreneurial Traits I Already Have" — gets finalized from four days of raw material (Day 1 self-inventory, Day 2 video insight, Day 3 value concept, Day 4 Key Activities draft) into one polished piece for the running Week 1–9 workbook section. This IS the deliverable — no new content today.
</div>

<h2 class="section">0:00–8:00 · Login &amp; Typing Practice</h2>
<p>Standard routine.</p>

<h2 class="section">8:00–11:00 · Week 1 Recap</h2>
<div class="script">
  <p class="t">This week, four things. Entrepreneur?</p>
  <p class="cr">"Takes on the risk, keeps the reward or the loss."</p>
  <p class="t">Key Resources?</p>
  <p class="cr">"Skills. And who you know."</p>
  <p class="t">Key Activities?</p>
  <p class="cr">"What they actually do."</p>
  <p class="t">And value?</p>
  <p class="cr">"Hard to get, and people agree it matters."</p>
  <p class="t">Today, all four of those become one thing — about you.</p>
</div>

<h2 class="section">11:00–29:00 · DI Instruction — Finalizing the Artifact</h2>

<h3 class="subsection">I Do — Modeling the Final Version</h3>
<div class="script">
  <p class="t">Here's what changes today. Monday you wrote a rough self-inventory. Since then we've added Key Activities and value. Now we combine them. Watch mine.</p>
  <p class="a">Model turning a Day 1 trait + Day 4 Key Activity into one combined, specific statement. Example: "I'm persistent (Day 1 trait) — every week I keep working with my Comp Apps students even when a lesson doesn't land the first time (Day 4 Key Activity). That persistence is valuable because not every teacher sticks with it (Day 3 value)."</p>
  <p class="t">Notice — I didn't just restate the trait. I proved it with something I actually do, and said why it's worth something.</p>
</div>

<h3 class="subsection">We Do — Peer Feedback Pass</h3>
<div class="script">
  <p class="t">Trade workbooks with a partner. Read their three traits. For each one — does it have a real example, or is it just a word? If it's just a word, ask them "what do you actually do that proves this?"</p>
  <p class="a">This is peer error-correction — the partner asks a question, they don't fill in the answer for their peer.</p>
</div>

<div class="note comprehension">
  <span class="label">Comprehension Tip</span>
  Watch for students who list a trait word (e.g., "hardworking") with no concrete Key Activity attached — that's the surface-level gap this whole week has been building toward closing. The peer-feedback question is designed to catch exactly this.
</div>

<h3 class="subsection">You Do — Final Write</h3>
<div class="script">
  <p class="t">Revise your three traits using your partner's feedback. This is the version that goes in your portfolio.</p>
</div>

<h2 class="section">29:00–40:00 · Voluntary Share-Out / Two Tracks</h2>

<h3 class="subsection">Main Class — Optional Share</h3>
<p>Ask for 3–4 volunteers to read one trait aloud. Not mandatory — some students will not be ready to share personal material publicly, and that's fine.</p>

<div class="sped-block">
  <h3 class="subsection">SPED Pull-Aside — Finishing the Week</h3>
  <p style="margin-top:0;"><strong>Format:</strong> One small group, teacher-led, 2nd-grade reading level.</p>
  <div class="sped-script">
    <p class="t">This week you told me: a skill you have, something you already do, and why it's worth something. Let's put it in one sentence. I do [skill], and that is worth something because [reason].</p>
    <p class="a">Help each student assemble their own sentence orally first, then support them writing or dictating it into the workbook.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<h2 class="section">40:00–45:00 · Exit Check + Preview Week 2</h2>
<div class="script">
  <p class="t">Log off in a minute — but first: next week we leave "who is an entrepreneur" and go to money. Real money — what things cost, what you have to give up to get something else. Come back ready to think about your own spending.</p>
  <p class="a">Not Yet / Developing / Fluent — final Week 1 fluency mark, based on the finished artifact quality.</p>
</div>

